  "replacements": [
    {
      "before": "For a nonempty $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, $u$ is an <strong>upper bound</strong> if $a\\le u$ for all $a\\in A$, and $A$ is <strong>bounded above</strong> if it has one. For such an $A$ the <strong>supremum</strong> $\\sup A$ is the least upper bound: (i) $a\\le\\sup A$ for all $a\\in A$, and (ii) for every $\\varepsilon\\gt0$ some $a\\in A$ has $a\\gt\\sup A-\\varepsilon$ (if $A$ is not bounded above, $\\sup A=+\\infty$, see the next box). If $\\sup A\\in A$ it is the <strong>maximum</strong>. Infimum (greatest lower bound) and minimum are symmetric, $\\inf A=-\\sup(-A)$, and $\\sup_{x\\in X}f(x):=\\sup f(X)$. <em>Completeness of $\\mathbb R$:</em> every nonempty set that is bounded above has a supremum in $\\mathbb R$.",
      "after": "For a nonempty $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, $u$ is an <strong>upper bound</strong> if $a\\le u$ for all $a\\in A$, and $A$ is <strong>bounded above</strong> if it has one. When $A$ is bounded above, its <strong>supremum</strong> $\\sup A$ is the least upper bound: (i) $a\\le\\sup A$ for all $a\\in A$, and (ii) for every $\\varepsilon\\gt0$ some $a\\in A$ has $a\\gt\\sup A-\\varepsilon$ (if $A$ is not bounded above, $\\sup A=+\\infty$, see the next box). If $\\sup A\\in A$ it is the <strong>maximum</strong>. Infimum (greatest lower bound) and minimum are symmetric, $\\inf A=-\\sup(-A)$, and $\\sup_{x\\in X}f(x):=\\sup f(X)$. <em>Completeness of $\\mathbb R$:</em> every nonempty set that is bounded above has a supremum in $\\mathbb R$.",
      "count": 1,
      "reason": "The upper/epsilon characterization requires a finite real supremum. Actual univ real counterdomain is nonempty and unbounded, its EReal supremum is +infinity, and no finite real a exceeds +infinity-epsilon. This adds the exact bounded-above guard while preserving the existing next-box convention."
    },
    {
      "before": "<p><strong>Example.</strong> SafeOpt starts from $C_0(x)=[h,\\infty)$ on the seed, so $l_0(x)=h$ and $u_0(x)=+\\infty$, and from $C_0(x)=\\mathbb R$ elsewhere ($l_0=-\\infty$). If data contradict an earlier band, $C_t(x)=\\emptyset$, and the conventions would give $l_t=+\\infty\\gt u_t=-\\infty$; <a href=\"safe-bo.html#safeopt-algorithm\">Module 4</a> treats this as a failed assumption, not as a certificate.</p>",
      "after": "<p><strong>Example.</strong> SafeOpt starts from $C_0(x)=[h,\\infty)$ on the seed, so $l_0(x)=h$ and $u_0(x)=+\\infty$, and from $C_0(x)=\\mathbb R$ elsewhere ($l_0=-\\infty$). If data contradict an earlier band, $C_t(x)=\\emptyset$, and the conventions would give $l_t=+\\infty\\gt u_t=-\\infty$; <a href=\"safe-bo.html#safeopt-algorithm\">Module 4</a> treats this as a failed confidence event or numerical error, not as a certificate; one empty intersection alone does not prove that the assumptions are false.</p>",
      "count": 1,
      "reason": "Exact linked Module4 line146 states failed confidence event or numerical error, and explicitly says assumptions can hold despite an allowed confidence-event failure. This aligns only the attribution; genuine extended band endpoint identities remain unchanged."
    },
    {
      "before": "<div>\"$\\max_{x\\in D}f(x)$\" presumes a maximizer: fine for finite $D$, or compact $D$ and continuous $f$ (Section 6). On $(0,1)$, $\\max x$ does not exist although $\\sup x=1$. A step \"pick $x_t\\in\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}$\" needs that existence, or an explicit approximate choice.</div>",
      "after": "<div>\"$\\max_{x\\in D}f(x)$\" presumes a maximizer: fine for nonempty finite $D$, or nonempty compact $D$ and continuous $f$ (Section 6). On $(0,1)$, $\\max x$ does not exist although $\\sup x=1$. A step \"pick $x_t\\in\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}$\" needs that existence, or an explicit approximate choice.</div>",
      "count": 1,
      "reason": "Actual finite/compact maximizer existence requires a nonempty domain. The empty domain is genuinely finite and compact and has empty argmax. This inserts only the missing nonempty qualification in both existence alternatives."
    }
  ],
